<h2>Science is most meaningful when we put it to work.</h2>
<p>My interests in biology, microscopy, wildlife, and the environment meet in the waterways around us. Water is the thread connecting wildlife, public health, recreation, and community life.</p>
<p>That is why I volunteer with <strong>Calusa Waterkeeper</strong>, a nonprofit devoted to protecting Southwest Florida waterways through monitoring, education, and advocacy. I serve as Vice President of the Board of Directors and help organize fecal indicator bacteria monitoring activities.</p>
<h2>From the field to the bigger picture</h2>
<p>Collecting a sample is only one step. Good monitoring also depends on careful procedures, reliable records, meaningful interpretation, and making results accessible. I value the way volunteers and scientists work together to ask questions and help communities better understand the waters around them.</p>
<p>I share this work because environmental protection starts with noticing, asking questions, and caring enough to act. I hope it inspires more people to explore our waters—and to help look after them.</p>
</div><aside class="side">
